# Function naming: `snake_case` (main library)

Applies to **main-library** C++ under **`base/`**, **`core/`**, **`content/`**, **`gfx/`**, and **`gui/`** (`.h` / `.hh` / `.cc` / `.cpp`). Does **not** apply to `third_party/` or vendored trees.

## Must be `snake_case`

Free functions, member functions (including `static`), and named callables the project owns.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD
void LoadConfig();
int layerCount() const;

// ✅ GOOD
void load_config();
int layer_count() const;
```

Boolean predicates prefer `is_` / `has_` / `can_` / `should_`.

## Rename policy (when editing)

If new or touched code in scope **encounters** a project-owned function named in `camelCase` or `PascalCase`:

1. Rename it to **`snake_case`** in the declaration and definition.
2. Update **all call sites inside the main library** in the **same change**.
3. Do **not** leave a local-only rename unless an external ABI forces it.

Do **not** drive-by rename unrelated symbols outside the edit’s blast radius.

## Out of scope

- **Types**: PascalCase (or match the file).
- **Third-party / STL / Win32 / WTL** names you call or override.
- Virtual overrides and COM/Win32 entry points whose name is fixed — keep the required name; prefer a thin `snake_case` helper when practical.
- Macros and test macros (`TEST`, `TEST_F`, `BENCHMARK`).
- Single-word STL-style members (`begin`, `end`, `size`, `empty`, `data`, `clear`).
